Here's a sample C++ program that reads a text file line by line and counts the number of occurrences of a specific word (in this case, "hello") in the file:

```
#include <iostream>
#include <fstream>
#include <string>

using namespace std;

int main()
{
    string filename = "myfile.txt";
    string searchWord = "hello";
    int count = 0;

    // Open the file
    ifstream file(filename);

    if (file.is_open())
    {
        // Read the file line by line
        string line;
        while (getline(file, line))
        {
            // Search for the word in the current line
            size_t pos = line.find(searchWord);
            while (pos != string::npos)
            {
                // Increment the count and search for the next occurrence of the word
                count++;
                pos = line.find(searchWord, pos + 1);
            }
        }

        // Close the file
        file.close();
    }
    else
    {
        cout << "Error opening file " << filename << endl;
    }

    // Output the count
    cout << "The word '" << searchWord << "' occurs " << count << " times in the file." << endl;

    return 0;
}
```

In this program, we first define the name of the text file we want to read (`filename`) and the word we want to search for (`searchWord`). We also initialize a counter variable (`count`) to 0.

We then open the file using an `ifstream` object and check if the file was successfully opened. If it was, we read the file line by line using a `while` loop and the `getline` function. Within each line, we search for the search word using the `find` function, which returns the position of the first occurrence of the word in the line (or `string::npos` if the word is not found).

If the word is found, we increment the count variable and continue searching for the next occurrence of the word by calling `find` again with a starting position one character after the last occurrence.

After we have searched the entire file, we output the count of occurrences of the search word.

Note that this program assumes that the search word appears as a separate word in the file, and not as part of another word (e.g. "hello" would be counted as a separate occurrence from "hello there"). If you want to count partial matches as well, you can modify the `find` function call accordingly.

